<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Burnt Palace at Kalhu";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/242008148%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/586620869%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/889344377%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/894019%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "The Burnt Palace, which was destroyed by fire when Kalhu fell to anti-Assyrian Babylonian and Median forces in 612 BC, has the longest building sequence yet identified at Nimrud (biblical Calah). This royal residence, which was constructed in the southeast quarter of the citadel, had at least nine phases of occupation between the Middle Assyrian and Hellenistic Periods. The so-called Burnt Palace was rebuilt/renovated three times during the ninth to seventh centuries BC and it might have temporarily served as a royal residence during the reign of Sargon II (r. 721–705 BC), while that Assyrian king was building his new capital Dūr-Šarrukīn. An immense collection of burnt ivories (the ‘Loftus ivories’) was discovered in and near the throne room in 1954–55; these are now in the British Museum (London).";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902/burnt-palace> dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:description "The ancient name of the building is not known. In the 1950s, British excavators dubbed this AssyrianPalace the Burnt Palace since its final, Assyrian building phase (the so-called “ivory level”) was destroyed by fire in 612 BC.";
    dcterms:modified "2021-01-18T11:34:47-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Assyrian";
    dcterms:title "Burnt Palace";
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902/burnt-palace> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902/imagery-location-of-the-burnt-palace> dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:description "Representative location of the Burnt Palace temple based on the visible ruins of the building.";
    dcterms:modified "2021-01-18T11:34:47-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Assyrian";
    dcterms:title "Imagery location of the Burnt Palace";
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902/imagery-location-of-the-burnt-palace> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902/south-east-palace> dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:description "The name originally given to this Assyrian palace by nineteen-century excavators. The modern designation was change to the Burnt Palace in order to avoid confusion with the building called the South-East Building / South-East Palace, located in the southeast corner of the Kalhu citadel.";
    dcterms:modified "2021-01-18T11:34:47-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Asclepius";
    dcterms:title "South-East Palace";
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/694530902/south-east-palace> .

