<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/62104824> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "ATAE Assur 15 (N 1) ",
        "Pedersén 1986 12–28",
        "Pedersén 1998 132";
    dcterms:contributor <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/ptushingham>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/tseidler>;
    dcterms:description "In the southwest courtyard and nearby rooms of the Aššur temple, researchers found a library and an archive consisting of at least 301 clay tablets, mostly fragmentary, and possibly several hundred more. These tablets were concentrated in the northwest part of the courtyard, the northwest rooms and the Enpi gate, and some stone tablets and other inscribed objects were also found in this area. Tablets found in other parts of the Aššur temple complex also belong to Assur Archive 15. This includes at least 72 clay tablets, often fragmentary, as well as fragmentary clay prisms and cylinders. These tablets were mostly found in the southern part of the large forecourt, as well as west of the temple complex. The texts included in the library date from the Old Assyrian to the Neo Assyrian period, with a core of Middle Assyrian texts and contained cultic and hymnic texts, myths, omens, incantations, prescriptions, lexical lists, as well as hemerological, mathematical, astronomical and astrological texts. The dating of the archive is similar; it contained lists, decrees, letters and historical texts.";
    dcterms:modified "2023-05-09T11:28:48-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Assyrian";
    dcterms:title "Assur Archive 15 (N 1)";
    cito:citesForInformation <http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/atae/assur_015>,
        <http://uu.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:88568/FULLTEXT01.pdf>,
        <https://www.zotero.org/groups/pleiades/items/2FXZ7SLX>;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/457530554>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/893945>;
    owl:sameAs <http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/62104824>;
    skos:altLabel "Assur Archive 15"@en,
        "N 1 Archive"@en;
    skos:inScheme <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places>;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom [ rdfs:label "Pleiades" ];
    pleiades:hasFeatureType <https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/archive-repository>;
    pleiades:hasLocation <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/plan-location-of-assur-archive-15>;
    pleiades:hasName <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/assur-archive-n1>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/n-01-archive> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/62104825%23this> a <http://geovocab.org/spatial#Feature>;
    rdfs:label "Assur Archive 18";
    spatial:C <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/637947000%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/881209103%23this>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/893945%23this>;
    rdfs:comment "At the eastern corner of the large ziggurat in Assur, a group of clay tablets were unearthed in a house in area hB4V. Forty clay tablets were found above a sarcophagus, 25 clay tablets were also located nearby and either some or all of them could belong to the first group. Additionally, a clay pot, containing 13 tablets, was found in a door opening of a different house. The texts seem to form a library, except for the tablets in the pot, which formed a separate archive (Assur Archive 51). The library, dating primarily to the 7th century BCE, belonged to a family of scribes and mainly contained documents pertaining to that profession: lexical lists, astronomical and astrological texts, omens, incantations, other literary texts, and drafts or copies of royal inscriptions.";
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/62104825> .

<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/621165832> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Paus. (Spiro: Perseus) 1.37.5",
        "Stewart 2012 p. 279 n. 30",
        "Wikidata Q16521555: Pythionike monument",
        "Wikipedia (Greek) Μνημείο της Πυθιονίκης";
    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jbecker>;
    dcterms:description "A cenotaph monument in western Attica praised by Pausanias, the Pythionike monument was located along the Sacred Way linking Athens and Eleusis. Stewart (2012) speculates whether Sulla could have plundered statuary from the monument when he marched on Athens.";
    dcterms:modified "2023-09-13T11:00:37-04:00";
    dcterms:title "Pythionike monument";
    cito:citesAsEvidence <http://data.perseus.org/citations/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0525.tlg001.perseus-grc1:1.37.5>;
    cito:citesAsRelated <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q16521555>;
